---
layout: default
title: ""
progress: true
---
<%
  release = latest_release
  post = release && release_post(release)
  runs = site.data.runs
  walk = site.data.walkthrough
  editor = site.data.editor
  grid = site.data.departments.select { |department| department[:plugin] == "rubocop" }.sort_by { |department| -department[:cops].size }
  counts = grid.flat_map { |department| department[:cops] }.map { |cop| cop[:status] }.tally
  indexed = site.data.cops.count { |cop| cop[:index] }
%>
<section class="hero" data-sec>
  <div>
    <h1>
      <%= render "logo" %>
    </h1>
    <p class="comment"># <%= site.metadata.tagline %></p>
    <p class="lede">RuboCop checks your Ruby code against the community <a href="<%= site.metadata.style_guide_url %>">Ruby Style Guide</a>, points out what's off and fixes most of it for you. It knows about bugs, style, layout and complexity, it bends to your team's taste, and it's been at it since 2012.</p>

    <div class="install">
      <div class="install-tabs" role="tablist">
        <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="install-line" data-cmd="gem install rubocop" data-prompt="$" data-note="">gem</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="install-line" data-cmd="gem 'rubocop', require: false" data-prompt="#" data-note="In your Gemfile. RuboCop is a standalone tool, so nothing needs to require it.">bundler</button>
      </div>
      <div class="install-line" id="install-line" role="tabpanel" aria-label="Install command"><span class="p">$</span><code>gem install rubocop</code><button type="button" class="copy link-button">copy</button></div>
    </div>
    <p class="install-note" id="install-note" hidden></p>
    <p class="comment extras"># optional: <a href="#project-index">rubydex</a> for cross-file checks, <a href="#agents">mcp</a> for agents</p>

    <p class="meta">
      <% if release %>
        Latest release: <a href="<%= release[:url] %>"><%= release[:version] %></a> (<%= release[:date].strftime("%-d %b %Y") %>)<% if post %>, and <a href="<%= post[:url] %>">the write-up</a><% end %>.
      <% else %>
        Latest release: <a href="<%= site.metadata.github_url %>/releases"><%= site.data.rubocop.version %></a>.
      <% end %>
      <br>
      New here? <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/getting_started.html">Getting started</a> takes five minutes, or <a href="<%= relative_url "/playground/" %>">try it in your browser</a> first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="RuboCop runs on a small project">
    <%= terminal_tabs "run", "rubocop" => runs[:lint], "rubocop --diff" => runs[:diff], "rubocop -a" => runs[:fix] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" id="cops" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>What it checks</h2>
    <p>Every check is a <em>cop</em>, and cops live in departments. <code>Lint</code> catches code that's probably a bug, <code>Style</code>, <code>Layout</code> and <code>Naming</code> keep a codebase consistent, <code>Metrics</code> flags methods and classes that got too big, and <code>Security</code> spots the usual footguns.</p>
    <p>Many of them come straight from a guideline in the style guide, and most can fix what they find.</p>
    <% if indexed.positive? %>
    <p id="project-index">Normally each cop sees one file at a time. Add the optional <code>rubydex</code> gem and set <code>UseProjectIndex: true</code> under <code>AllCops</code>, and RuboCop indexes the whole project first. The index serves <a href="<%= relative_url "/cops/" %>?index=yes"><%= indexed %> cops</a>: <code>Lint/DuplicateMethods</code> catches a method defined twice in different files, others stop flagging code that's fine once they can see the rest of the project, and a few new ones, like <code>Lint/NameTypo</code>, only work with it. It's experimental and needs Ruby 3.2 or newer.</p>
    <% end %>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="<%= relative_url "/cops/" %>">Browse all the cops &rarr;</a>
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/project_index.html">The project index &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="cops-grid" data-src="<%= relative_url "/cops.json" %>">
    <div class="cops-head">Inspecting <%= counts.values.sum %> cops</div>
    <% grid.each do |department| %>
      <div class="dept" data-name="<%= department[:name] %>" data-fixable="<%= department[:cops].count { |cop| cop[:autocorrect] } %>">
        <span class="dept-name"><a href="<%= department[:url] %>"><%= department[:name] %>/</a></span>
        <span class="dept-n"><%= department[:cops].size %></span>
        <span class="cells"><% department[:cops].each do |cop| %><a href="<%= cop[:url] %>" class="<%= cop[:status] %>" aria-label="<%= cop[:name] %>"></a><% end %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <div class="legend"><span class="sq">on by default</span><span class="sq pending">pending (on in the next major release)</span><span class="sq disabled">opt-in</span></div>
    <div class="readout" aria-live="polite">Each square is a cop. <%= counts["enabled"] %> are on by default, <%= counts["pending"] %> are pending until the next major release and <%= counts["disabled"] %> are opt-in. Hover one, or tab in and use the arrow keys, to see what it does.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>What it fixes</h2>
    <p><code>rubocop -a</code> applies the corrections that can't change what your code does. <code>rubocop -A</code> goes further and applies the ones that might, so run it with your tests nearby.</p>
    <p>Want to look before you leap? <code>--diff</code> prints the patch and leaves your files alone. The <code>Layout</code> cops make RuboCop a formatter too, and <code>rubocop -x</code> runs just those.</p>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="<%= relative_url "/playground/" %>">Try it on your own code &rarr;</a>
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/autocorrect.html">How autocorrection works &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="term">
    <%= terminal runs[:fix_all] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Make it yours</h2>
    <p>The defaults follow the style guide, but Ruby has never had one true style and RuboCop doesn't pretend otherwise. Many cops have settings, over a hundred of them let you pick a style, and it's all set in a single <code>.rubocop.yml</code>, which can inherit from a shared one.</p>
    <p>Adopting it on an old codebase? <code>rubocop --auto-gen-config</code> records every existing offense in <code>.rubocop_todo.yml</code>, so new code meets the standard today and the old debt gets paid off one cop at a time.</p>
    <p>For real-world examples, see how <a href="https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/main/.rubocop.yml">Rails</a>, <a href="https://github.com/gitlabhq/gitlabhq/blob/master/.rubocop.yml">GitLab</a>, <a href="https://github.com/discourse/discourse/blob/main/.rubocop.yml">Discourse</a> and <a href="https://github.com/mastodon/mastodon/blob/main/.rubocop.yml">Mastodon</a> set it up.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>configuration.html">Configuration &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="yaml">
<pre tabindex="0"><span class="y-c"># .rubocop.yml</span>
<span class="y-k">inherit_from</span>: <span class="y-s">.rubocop_todo.yml</span>

<span class="y-k">AllCops</span>:
  <span class="y-k">TargetRubyVersion</span>: <span class="y-v">3.4</span>
  <span class="y-k">NewCops</span>: <span class="y-v">enable</span>

<span class="y-k">Style/StringLiterals</span>:
  <span class="y-k">EnforcedStyle</span>: <span class="y-v">double_quotes</span>   <span class="y-c"># we know, we know</span>

<span class="y-k">Metrics/MethodLength</span>:
  <span class="y-k">Max</span>: <span class="y-v">15</span>
  <span class="y-k">Exclude</span>:
    - <span class="y-s">db/migrate/**/*</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>RuboCop never runs your code. It parses each file into a syntax tree, with Prism, or with the parser gem for code that targets Ruby older than 3.3. Then it walks the tree once and calls each cop for the kinds of nodes it asked to see.</p>
    <p>Many cops are little more than a node pattern and a message. <code>Style/NilComparison</code> asks to see calls to <code>==</code>, <code>===</code> and <code>nil?</code>, and reports the ones its pattern matches.</p>
    <p>Corrections don't touch the tree. A cop rewrites the source where its node came from, and RuboCop parses the result and runs the cops again, until a pass changes nothing. That's how one <code>rubocop -a</code> can sort out code that takes several cops in turn.</p>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop-ast/latest/node_pattern.html">Node patterns &rarr;</a>
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>development.html">Writing your own cops &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="term walk" role="group" aria-label="One offense followed through RuboCop">
    <%= terminal_tabs "walk", "parse" => walk[:parse], "match" => walk[:match], "report" => runs[:walk], "correct" => runs[:walk_fix] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Plugins</h2>
    <p>The core knows Ruby. Plugins teach it about the frameworks and libraries around it. The RuboCop team maintains the ones on the right, and there are plenty more out there, from <code>rubocop-graphql</code> to <code>rubocop-sorbet</code>.</p>
    <p>Writing your own cops is easier than it sounds, and a project can keep a few in-house cops for its own conventions.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>plugins.html">All the plugins &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="yaml">
    <% width = site.data.plugins.map { |plugin| plugin[:name].size }.max %>
<pre tabindex="0"><span class="y-k">plugins</span>:
<% site.data.plugins.each do |plugin| %>  - <a class="y-s" href="https://github.com/rubocop/<%= plugin[:name] %>"><%= plugin[:name] %></a><%= " " * (width - plugin[:name].size) %>  <span class="y-c"># <%= plugin[:note] %></span>
<% end %></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>In your editor</h2>
    <p>RuboCop has a language server built in. Your editor gets offenses as you type, a quick fix for each one, and RuboCop's corrections as a formatter, which most editors can run on save. It's the same RuboCop with the same config your CI runs.</p>
    <p>Ruby LSP, which the docs recommend for most editors, runs RuboCop for you. Otherwise point your editor's LSP client at <code>rubocop --lsp</code>, or install the vscode-rubocop extension. RubyMine has RuboCop support of its own.</p>
    <p>For editors that just shell out to <code>rubocop</code>, <code>--server</code> keeps a warm process around, so each run starts instantly. It isn't available on Windows or JRuby.</p>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>integration_with_other_tools.html#editor-integration">Editor setup &rarr;</a>
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/lsp.html">The language server &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="term lsp" role="group" aria-label="<%= editor[:file] %> in an editor running rubocop --lsp">
    <span class="lsp-file" aria-hidden="true"><%= editor[:file] %></span>
    <%= editor_tabs "ed", "as you type" => editor[:typing], "after saving" => editor[:saved] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" id="agents" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>In your agent</h2>
    <p>Coding agents run RuboCop far more often than people do, and everything it prints ends up in their context. <code>rubocop --mcp</code> gives them inspection, autocorrection and cop explanations as tools, answered by a RuboCop that's already loaded. It needs the <code>mcp</code> gem.</p>
    <p>From the shell, <code>--format json</code> tells an agent which offenses autocorrection can fix and the edits it would make, <code>--explain</code> tells it what a cop wants when the message isn't enough, and <code>--max-offenses-per-cop</code> keeps an old codebase from flooding its context.</p>
    <p>An agent told to make RuboCop pass can disable its way there. Enable <a href="<%= site.data.cop_urls["Style/DisableCopsWithinSourceCodeDirective"] %>">the cop that polices <code>rubocop:disable</code> comments</a> with <code>AllowWithReason</code>, and every one of them needs a reason, so whoever reviews the change sees why.</p>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/ai_agents.html">RuboCop and coding agents &rarr;</a>
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/mcp.html">The MCP server &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="term">
    <%= terminal_tabs "agent", "-f json" => runs[:json], "--explain" => runs[:explain] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>In CI</h2>
    <p>Offenses can land right on the pull request. <code>--format github</code> turns them into annotations, <code>sarif</code> feeds code scanning and <code>junit</code> the rest of CI. <code>--fail-level</code> decides which severities fail the build, and <code>--changed=main...</code> checks only the files a branch changed since it forked.</p>
    <p>When there's too much to read, <code>-f offenses</code> and <code>-f worst</code> show where the offenses pile up.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>formatters.html">Formatters &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="term">
    <%= terminal_tabs "format", "-f github" => runs[:github], "-f offenses" => runs[:offenses], "-f worst" => runs[:worst] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" id="news" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>News</h2>
    <p>A new release lands every few weeks. The <a href="<%= site.metadata.changelog_url %>">changelog</a> has every detail, and the bigger ones get a write-up on <a href="https://metaredux.com/tags/#rubocop">Meta Redux</a>.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="log news">
    <% news.each do |item| %>
      <li>
        <span class="d"><%= item[:date].iso8601 %></span>
        <% if item[:kind] == "post" %>
          <span><a href="<%= item[:url] %>"><%= item[:title] %></a><span class="tag">post</span></span>
        <% else %>
          <span>
            <a href="<%= item[:url] %>"><%= item[:version] %></a><span class="sum">: <%= item[:summary] %></span>
            <% if item[:new_cops].any? %>
              <br><span class="sum">new: <%== item[:new_cops].first(3).map { |name| cop_link(name) }.join(", ") %><%= ", ..." if item[:new_cops].size > 3 %></span>
            <% end %>
          </span>
        <% end %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>I started RuboCop in April 2012 to make it easy to actually follow the <a href="<%= site.metadata.style_guide_url %>">Ruby Style Guide</a>, which I'd started a few months earlier. <%= in_words(years_since(site.data.history.first[:date])).capitalize %> years, two parsers and over <%= site.data.contributors ? delimited(site.data.contributors / 100 * 100) : "a thousand" %> contributors later, it's how a big chunk of the Ruby world keeps its code in shape.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>about/history.html">The longer version &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <ul class="log">
    <% site.data.history.each do |event| %>
      <li>
        <% if event[:commit] %><a class="h" href="<%= event[:url] %>"><%= event[:commit] %></a><% else %><span class="h">-------</span><% end %>
        <span class="d"><%= event[:date].iso8601 %></span>
        <span><%= code_spans event[:text] %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="s full" id="funding" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
    <p>RuboCop is free and maintained by a small core team of volunteers, with help from thousands of contributors. If your company's code goes through RuboCop every day, consider giving something back. It's what keeps the lights on and the releases coming.</p>
    <ul class="people">
      <% site.data.team.each do |member| %>
        <li><img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/<%= member[:github] %>?s=60" alt="" loading="lazy" width="30" height="30"><a href="https://github.com/<%= member[:github] %>"><%= member[:name] %></a></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
    <div class="fund">
      <a href="https://opencollective.com/rubocop"><b>Open Collective</b><span>The project's own fund, for individuals and companies.</span></a>
      <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/bbatsov"><b>GitHub Sponsors</b><span>Sponsor the maintainers directly.</span></a>
      <a href="https://tidelift.com/subscription/pkg/rubygems-rubocop"><b>Tidelift</b><span>For companies that want it on an invoice.</span></a>
    </div>
    <% { organizations: "organizations backing RuboCop on Open Collective", individuals: "and individuals" }.each do |kind, label| %>
      <% next if site.data.backers[kind].to_a.empty? %>
      <p class="backers-label"># <%= label %></p>
      <div class="backers <%= kind %>">
        <% site.data.backers[kind].each do |backer| %>
          <a href="<%= backer[:url] %>" rel="noopener" title="<%= backer[:name] %>"><img src="<%= backer[:image] %>" alt="<%= backer[:name] %>" loading="lazy" width="36" height="36"></a>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
